-- T-FIELD-VARIANT-CONFLICT, the IDENTICAL-LAYOUT arm (#1455 review round 2).
--
-- `Pair`'s two constructors agree on `px`'s offset — they agree on every offset — and
-- the selection is STILL refused. That is the half of the rule that is easy to get
-- wrong, and the first cut of this fix did get it wrong: it exempted agreeing offsets
-- on the argument that any admissible stamp then emits the same `loadField`.
--
-- 🚨 THAT ARGUMENT IS TRUE OF THE LLVM BACKEND AND FALSE OF WasmGC, AND CI SAID SO.
-- `gates (engines)` went red on a fixture built from exactly this exemption. On WasmGC
-- a record is a `struct` type PER CONSTRUCTOR, so the stamp names a TYPE and the access
-- is a cast: casting a `PB` value to `PA`'s struct type is illegal whatever the layouts
-- are. MEASURED on cold builds of BOTH the merge base `bfcd4ea7` and this branch — so
-- the divergence is pre-existing, and what changed is only that the front end stopped
-- accepting the shape:
--
--     |PA{px,py}|PB{px,py,pz}| , mkA.px and mkB.px   native pa=1,2 …   wasm: illegal cast
--     |PA{px,py}|PB{px,py}|    , equal arity too     native p=1,3      wasm: illegal cast
--     |A{x}|B{x}|              , IDENTICAL fields    native t=1,2      wasm: illegal cast
--
-- The third row was `test/references_fixtures/dup_field_def/`'s own `getX t = t.x`,
-- which this PR had to rewrite as a `match` for the same reason.
--
-- So the rule carries no offset exemption: a `.field` whose receiver TYPE has two
-- named-field constructors declaring that field names no constructor, and the offsets
-- appear only in the message. Its sibling `field_variant_offset_conflict.mdk` is the
-- DIFFERING-offset arm, which gets the sharper of the two messages.
data Pair =
  | PA { px : Int, py : Int }
  | PB { px : Int, py : Int, pz : Int }

getPx : Pair -> Int
getPx p = p.px
